#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""afc-council: a group chat of every signed-in LLM and the agents you make, on the topic you raise.

Alt+K in the fleet opens it in a tab of its own, in the folder of the pane you were in: the tab is
dealt into an even grid, one pane per member (each its own scroll of the thread, a spinner while it
thinks), with this chat in a strip along the bottom where you type. Claude, Codex,
Gemini and Antigravity sit at the table (each on the account it starts on, `agents --accounts`, when
that account is signed in), and so does every agent you have made: a name, a persona, and the LLM
it runs on. Each member reads the whole thread before it speaks and is told to give its own honest,
raw take: no flattery, no hedging, disagree by name.

  You type             everyone answers (in the mode below)
  @gemini @skeptic …   only those answer; @all is everyone
  Enter on an empty line, or /again   another round on the same message: they answer each other

  /format forum|bp|stake   how the meeting runs (/format alone steps to the next; kept per project):
                       forum = Open Forum, the modes below, ending after /rounds;
                       bp    = British Parliamentary debate on your message (the motion): up to 8 members
                               as PM, LO, DPM, DLO, MG, MO, GW, OW in that order, then the judge ranks
                               the four benches (OG, OO, CG, CO);
                       stake = Win Stake: everyone tries to win everyone over, /rounds rounds (at most
                               12), then each votes for who convinced it most; the judge announces the
                               winner and sums up the top 3.
                       /bp: the BP speakers, four benches of two, each with its model; set by
                       seat unless chosen (/bp ROLE @name, /bp auto); the menu opens with /format bp.
                       The judge: a member with the judge power, else one with the remove power
                       (Supra Omnia); it sits the debate out. With none, the council counts the votes.
  /mode                switch between the two modes below (/order and /roast go straight to one);
                       Shift+Tab does it too, and Alt+P steps the permission: plan, edit, bypass
  /mode order          everyone answers at once (each its own take), then round the table one by one,
                       each speaking to the others' answers (the default)
  /mode roast          everyone gives a point at once, then they roast each other, round after round,
                       each answering what was just said about it
  /rounds N            order mode: passes round the table after the answers (default 2; 0 = until Ctrl+C
                       or everyone passes; none = answers only); roast mode: roast rounds per message
                       (default 2; 0 = until Ctrl+C)
  /names model|llm     what an LLM goes by: the model it runs on (@opus, @gpt-6-astra; the default) or
                       its own name (@claude); agents with a character keep theirs. Kept per project
  /length N            answers of at most N words (default 60: two or three sentences); kept
  /topic TEXT          what the meeting is about (else your first message is)
  /agent new NAME LLM CHARACTER [|| SOUL || RULES]   make an agent (its soul, character, rules and memory
                       go in <project>/agents/NAME/, read before every answer; edit them there). LLM: claude | codex | gemini | agy | copilot | opencode, with an
                       account and a model if you like: claude@others:opus, codex:gpt-5.5
  /lean @name opposition|government|free   where a member stands (any member, an LLM's seat too; @all
                       for everyone): always against the chair, for the chair and its agenda, or no side
                       (free will, the default). Alt+M on its pane: O, G, F. /agent lean NAME … the same
  /agent power NAME remove [on|off]   let an agent give strikes on its own judgement (it ends an
                       answer with "Strike: @name - why"); at 3 strikes the member is removed: an agent
                       is deleted and its memory wiped, an LLM's own seat leaves the table. /strikes
                       shows them, /strikes clear [@name] wipes them
  /agent power NAME judge [on|off]    a judge sits each round out and gives its verdict when it ends
  /agent del NAME      remove one       /agents   who is at the table, and on what
  /bench NAME, /seat NAME   take a member off the table for now, or back (also an LLM: /seat agy)
  /remove all          everyone off the table, as ✕ on each would (+ add: Remove everyone…)
  /remove NAME         what ✕ on a member's header does: an agent you made is deleted for good (from
                       every council; + add makes a new one); an LLM's own seat is benched
  /model @name MODEL   the model that member runs on (@all for everyone; `default` to go back)
  /effort @name LEVEL  how hard it thinks: low medium high xhigh max (Codex: minimal..xhigh;
                       Gemini: low..xhigh, one setting for every Gemini, not one per member).
                       /model or /effort alone: who runs on what. /models: the names to pick from.
                       Kept for the next meeting.
  /perm plan|edit|bypass   what members may do in this folder: plan reads only (the default), edit
                       may change files but runs no command, bypass may do anything with no asking.
                       /perm @name … for one member (also in Alt+M on its pane); shown in the headers
  /view one|grid       one pane with everyone's answers (yours on the right), or one pane per member
                       (the default). Alt+O in the chat box switches; kept per project
  /grid on|off         one pane per member, each with its own scroll of the thread (on by itself
                       in the council's own tab); off: every answer in full in this pane
  /new [TOPIC]         a new meeting, in a folder of its own (the old one stays saved; Alt+T: what is typed is its
                       topic). A council opened in a folder carries on that folder's last meeting: everyone remembers it
  /resume              carry on an earlier meeting instead (a list; /resume N picks one)
  /retry [@name]       ask again a member whose run went wrong (all of those, without a name; @ is
                       optional). Failures are reported on the note line with what to do next
  /sim N [QUESTION]    Monte Carlo simulation: run N independent trials (1 to 20) in the current format
                       (Open Forum, BP Debate, or Win Stake), each isolated from prior trials, and
                       tally win rates, vote totals, and agreement (omit QUESTION to re-run the last)
  /quit                leave (Ctrl+D too). Ctrl+C while they answer stops that round.

Members only read: files in this folder (and the web, where the LLM can), never a change. Each answer
is one non-interactive run of that LLM's own program, so it counts against that account's plan like
any chat. Before each run the fleet's memory walls are asked (`agents --room`); a member that would
not fit is skipped and the thread says why. Roast mode runs up to `parallel` members at once
(default 3).

Where it is kept. Each meeting is a folder of its own, made when the council opens and named by the
time and, from the first message, its topic: <project>/logs/council/<date>_<time>_<topic>/, with
thread.md to read and thread.json to carry it on (logs/council/ is ignored by git, like logs/acf). A
meeting nobody spoke in leaves no folder. A council about no project (Alt+Shift+K) keeps its meetings
in ~/Council/<date>_<time>_<topic>/, and its members work in that folder. Each project's settings
(mode, permission, models, efforts, who sits at the table) are its own: <project>/logs/council/
settings.json (~/Council/settings.json); a project's council starts read-only (plan), whatever another
project's is set to. Made agents are shared by every council: ~/.config/agents/council.json.

  afc-council [--dir FOLDER] [--from-pid PID]     the chat (Alt+K starts it with the pane's pid)
  afc-council --open <session> <pane pid>         what Alt+K runs: a new tab with the chat in it
  afc-council --general | --open-general <session>  a council about no project, in ~/Council (Alt+Shift+K)
"""
